1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,510
Berlin. The ninth of July, 2006. The World Cup final.

2
00:00:06,060 --> 00:00:11,936
Penalties. Italy against France. Four Italian kicks. Four goals.

3
00:00:11,986 --> 00:00:16,380
And now Fabio Grosso steps up to take the fifth.

4
00:00:16,930 --> 00:00:22,760
He scores. Italy are champions of the world, for the fourth time in their history.

5
00:00:23,310 --> 00:00:29,140
Their captain, Fabio Cannavaro, lifts the trophy into the Berlin sky.

6
00:00:29,690 --> 00:00:37,003
Behind him celebrate Gianluigi Buffon, Alessandro Del Piero, Mauro Camoranesi and

7
00:00:37,053 --> 00:00:38,730
Gianluca Zambrotta.

8
00:00:39,280 --> 00:00:43,670
Five of these world champions play for the same club. Juventus.

9
00:00:44,220 --> 00:00:49,435
But more than a thousand kilometres away, in Rome, a tribunal is preparing a

10
00:00:49,485 --> 00:00:53,730
verdict that will send their club down to the second division.

11
00:00:54,280 --> 00:01:00,535
Just five days after the greatest night in modern Italian football came one of its

12
00:01:00,585 --> 00:01:01,150
darkest.

13
00:01:01,700 --> 00:01:08,821
This is the story of the summer Italian football was crowned… and convicted.

14
00:01:08,871 --> 00:01:11,840
This is the story of Calciopoli.

15
00:01:21,390 --> 00:01:25,325
In 1994, Juventus appointed a new general manager.

16
00:01:25,375 --> 00:01:29,390
He had never been a star player, or a famous coach.

17
00:01:29,940 --> 00:01:35,692
He had started out working at a railway station, then became a talent scout, and

18
00:01:35,742 --> 00:01:39,972
climbed all the way to the top of Italy's most famous club.

19
00:01:40,022 --> 00:01:41,930
His name was Luciano Moggi.

20
00:01:42,480 --> 00:01:45,962
Moggi had a rare gift. He knew everyone.

21
00:01:46,012 --> 00:01:51,260
Agents, coaches, journalists, club presidents… and referees.

22
00:01:51,810 --> 00:01:58,520
With chief executive Antonio Giraudo and former club legend Roberto Bettega, Moggi

23
00:01:58,570 --> 00:02:02,478
formed what the Italian press called the Triade.

24
00:02:02,528 --> 00:02:06,600
Three men running the biggest club in the country.

25
00:02:07,150 --> 00:02:09,282
The results were spectacular.

26
00:02:09,332 --> 00:02:15,376
League titles, Champions League finals, and transfers that made Juventus the club

27
00:02:15,426 --> 00:02:16,580
everyone feared.

28
00:02:17,130 --> 00:02:23,666
But in Italian football, one question echoed in every café and on every TV talk

29
00:02:23,716 --> 00:02:26,250
show. Who chooses the referees?

30
00:02:26,800 --> 00:02:33,021
At the time, two men were responsible for assigning referees to Serie A matches:

31
00:02:33,071 --> 00:02:35,921
Paolo Bergamo and Pierluigi Pairetto.

32
00:02:35,971 --> 00:02:40,310
Every week, they decided who would officiate which game.

33
00:02:40,860 --> 00:02:46,017
And in a league where titles could be decided by a single point, the choice of a

34
00:02:46,067 --> 00:02:47,970
referee could mean everything.

35
00:02:48,520 --> 00:02:55,876
On the sixth of November 2004, Juventus lost 2–1 away to Reggina, a small club from

36
00:02:55,926 --> 00:03:01,140
the far south of Italy. The referee was Gianluca Paparesta.

37
00:03:01,690 --> 00:03:06,890
It was a famous upset. But the real story happened after the final whistle.

38
00:03:07,440 --> 00:03:13,062
Two years later, Italians would hear a recording of Moggi boasting about what he

39
00:03:13,112 --> 00:03:16,891
did after that match. That he had berated the referee…

40
00:03:16,941 --> 00:03:21,500
then locked him in his dressing room and walked off with the key.

41
00:03:22,050 --> 00:03:26,146
And the strangest part? Moggi did not say it to a friend.

42
00:03:26,196 --> 00:03:32,110
He said it to Maria Grazia Fazi, the secretary of the referees' commission itself.

43
00:03:32,660 --> 00:03:35,067
Moggi later called it just a joke.

44
00:03:35,117 --> 00:03:40,775
A court in Reggio Calabria closed the investigation, ruling it had been nothing

45
00:03:40,825 --> 00:03:42,075
more than a boast.

46
00:03:42,125 --> 00:03:47,711
But the recording survived, and became one of the most infamous moments of the

47
00:03:47,761 --> 00:03:48,650
whole affair.

48
00:03:49,200 --> 00:03:52,860
What Moggi did not know at the time was that someone was listening.

49
00:03:57,410 --> 00:04:00,383
The story did not begin with referees.

50
00:04:00,433 --> 00:04:04,440
It began with a completely different investigation.

51
00:04:04,990 --> 00:04:11,866
In Turin, prosecutors were looking into Juventus, and into a players' agency called

52
00:04:11,916 --> 00:04:14,370
GEA World, run by Moggi's son.

53
00:04:14,420 --> 00:04:19,460
Between August and September 2004, phones began to be tapped.

54
00:04:20,010 --> 00:04:23,953
The files were later taken up by prosecutors in Naples.

55
00:04:24,003 --> 00:04:27,510
And there, the case became something much bigger.

56
00:04:28,060 --> 00:04:33,017
What investigators found was not one phone call. It was a network.

57
00:04:33,067 --> 00:04:38,935
Club executives, referee designators and federation officials, talking to each

58
00:04:38,985 --> 00:04:40,680
other, again and again.

59
00:04:41,230 --> 00:04:46,446
When Moggi suspected his phones might be monitored, he turned to a solution:

60
00:04:46,496 --> 00:04:51,990
foreign SIM cards, bought in the Swiss border town of Chiasso, and handed to the

61
00:04:52,040 --> 00:04:53,930
people he needed to talk to.

62
00:04:54,480 --> 00:04:59,716
Moggi would later say the cards were only for keeping transfers secret, and that

63
00:04:59,766 --> 00:05:04,540
they had been the club's idea, not his. Either way, they were not enough.

64
00:05:05,090 --> 00:05:08,086
The investigation did not stop at Juventus.

65
00:05:08,136 --> 00:05:13,400
Names from Milan, Fiorentina, Lazio and Reggina appeared in the recordings.

66
00:05:13,950 --> 00:05:19,150
In total, magistrates in Naples investigated forty-one people.

67
00:05:19,700 --> 00:05:23,390
Then, in May 2006, everything exploded.

68
00:05:23,440 --> 00:05:30,006
Italian newspapers began publishing the transcripts. Page after page.

69
00:05:30,056 --> 00:05:31,540
Name after name.

70
00:05:32,090 --> 00:05:37,608
Italians read, in the officials' own words, how referees were discussed…

71
00:05:37,658 --> 00:05:39,850
and how things were arranged.

72
00:05:40,400 --> 00:05:42,881
This was not one phone call, or ten.

73
00:05:42,931 --> 00:05:47,872
The investigations covered tens of thousands of recorded conversations.

74
00:05:47,922 --> 00:05:52,160
And what was published was enough to shake an entire country.

75
00:05:52,710 --> 00:05:58,089
But according to the recordings, Moggi's influence did not stop at referees.

76
00:05:58,139 --> 00:05:59,660
It reached television.

77
00:06:00,210 --> 00:06:05,789
In December 2004, Juventus beat Bologna 1–0 in a match full of refereeing

78
00:06:05,839 --> 00:06:06,714
controversy.

79
00:06:06,764 --> 00:06:12,574
The calls later showed Moggi talking to Fabio Baldas, the former referee who

80
00:06:12,624 --> 00:06:16,738
analysed replays on Italy's most famous football show.

81
00:06:16,788 --> 00:06:23,138
The aim, according to investigators: to present the replays in a way that would not

82
00:06:23,188 --> 00:06:27,070
expose the referee's mistakes in Juventus's favour.

83
00:06:27,620 --> 00:06:33,370
The show's host, Aldo Biscardi, was suspended from journalism for six months.

84
00:06:33,920 --> 00:06:35,995
And it was not only Juventus.

85
00:06:36,045 --> 00:06:41,418
At Milan, Leonardo Meani was the man in charge of relations with referees.

86
00:06:41,468 --> 00:06:46,987
The recordings revealed his interest in one assistant referee in particular:

87
00:06:47,037 --> 00:06:48,160
Claudio Puglisi.

88
00:06:48,710 --> 00:06:55,285
In one call, the designator Paolo Bergamo explained to Pairetto why Puglisi had

89
00:06:55,335 --> 00:07:01,490
been assigned to Milan against Chievo. "There was some pressure," he said.

90
00:07:02,040 --> 00:07:08,536
And in May 2005, Meani asked Adriano Galliani, Milan's chief executive and, at the

91
00:07:08,586 --> 00:07:13,246
same time, president of the league itself, to back Puglisi.

92
00:07:13,296 --> 00:07:16,280
Galliani's reply: "Sure, that's fine."

93
00:07:16,830 --> 00:07:22,216
On the eighth of May, the president of the Italian Football Federation, Franco

94
00:07:22,266 --> 00:07:23,470
Carraro, resigned.

95
00:07:24,020 --> 00:07:28,410
On the eleventh of May, the entire Juventus board resigned.

96
00:07:28,960 --> 00:07:34,632
And on the fourteenth of May, Juventus won the league title, for the second season

97
00:07:34,682 --> 00:07:35,260
in a row.

98
00:07:35,810 --> 00:07:42,222
That same day, Luciano Moggi resigned, with a line Italians would never forget.

99
00:07:42,272 --> 00:07:44,430
"They have killed my soul."

100
00:07:44,980 --> 00:07:51,149
The next morning, La Gazzetta dello Sport put it on its front page.

101
00:07:51,199 --> 00:07:54,490
"Juve win their 29th title… or not?"

102
00:07:55,040 --> 00:08:00,060
A title celebrated on the pitch… and a director leaving through the back door.

103
00:08:00,610 --> 00:08:06,360
And through all of this… the players had to travel to Germany. For the World Cup.

104
00:08:10,910 --> 00:08:14,448
Italy arrived in Germany in the middle of a storm.

105
00:08:14,498 --> 00:08:20,190
At home, news from the courts was taking up more space than news about the team.

106
00:08:20,740 --> 00:08:23,041
Many expected this team to collapse.

107
00:08:23,091 --> 00:08:28,395
How could players focus on a World Cup while their clubs waited for a verdict that

108
00:08:28,445 --> 00:08:29,440
could ruin them?

109
00:08:29,990 --> 00:08:35,710
Coach Marcello Lippi, a man who had spent years in charge of Juventus, decided to

110
00:08:35,760 --> 00:08:41,622
turn the pressure into fuel. The message was simple. The whole world is against us.

111
00:08:41,672 --> 00:08:43,260
Let's prove them wrong.

112
00:08:43,810 --> 00:08:46,829
And match after match, Italy kept going.

113
00:08:46,879 --> 00:08:52,430
A defence that would not break, led by Cannavaro, with Buffon behind him.

114
00:08:52,980 --> 00:08:58,536
In the semi-final, they knocked out the hosts, Germany, in Dortmund, with two goals

115
00:08:58,586 --> 00:09:00,900
in the final minutes of extra time.

116
00:09:01,450 --> 00:09:05,270
And in the final, France. Zidane's France.

117
00:09:05,820 --> 00:09:12,120
A match remembered forever for Zinedine Zidane's headbutt on Marco Materazzi, and

118
00:09:12,170 --> 00:09:15,570
the red card in the last game of his career.

119
00:09:16,120 --> 00:09:20,376
Then came penalties. Pirlo. Materazzi. De Rossi.

120
00:09:20,426 --> 00:09:25,400
Then Del Piero, the Juventus captain, scored the fourth.

121
00:09:25,950 --> 00:09:32,033
And the twist? The only penalty missed all night belonged to David Trezeguet.

122
00:09:32,083 --> 00:09:37,370
France's striker… and a Juventus player. His shot hit the crossbar.

123
00:09:37,920 --> 00:09:40,460
Italy were world champions.

124
00:09:41,010 --> 00:09:44,360
In Rome, millions poured into the streets.

125
00:09:44,910 --> 00:09:49,837
Three days after the final, Marcello Lippi stunned everyone.

126
00:09:49,887 --> 00:09:52,326
He resigned as national coach.

127
00:09:52,376 --> 00:09:59,212
Lippi himself was not under investigation, but his son Davide was, over his work at

128
00:09:59,262 --> 00:10:06,180
the GEA agency. Lippi said he felt that he and his son had been attacked personally.

129
00:10:06,730 --> 00:10:10,230
Two days later… the news arrived from Rome.

130
00:10:14,780 --> 00:10:20,831
On the fourteenth of July 2006, five days after Berlin, the sports tribunal

131
00:10:20,881 --> 00:10:22,620
delivered its verdict.

132
00:10:23,170 --> 00:10:29,230
Juventus: relegated to Serie B for the first time in their history, with a

133
00:10:29,280 --> 00:10:35,010
thirty-point penalty, and stripped of the 2005 and 2006 league titles.

134
00:10:35,560 --> 00:10:42,670
Fiorentina and Lazio: relegated too. Milan and Reggina: points deductions.

135
00:10:43,220 --> 00:10:48,420
Fiorentina's owner, Diego Della Valle, rejected the accusations outright.

136
00:10:48,470 --> 00:10:52,808
"The truth is that Fiorentina was the victim of these things…

137
00:10:52,858 --> 00:10:56,260
and has absolutely nothing to do with all this."

138
00:10:56,810 --> 00:11:01,670
The shock was enormous. And the appeals began immediately.

139
00:11:02,220 --> 00:11:07,005
After two rounds of appeals, the punishments were softened.

140
00:11:07,055 --> 00:11:12,005
Fiorentina and Lazio stayed in Serie A with point deductions.

141
00:11:12,055 --> 00:11:18,561
In October, Juventus's penalty was cut to nine points… but the relegation stood.

142
00:11:18,611 --> 00:11:20,610
And the titles were gone.

143
00:11:21,160 --> 00:11:25,091
The 2005 title was left without a champion.

144
00:11:25,141 --> 00:11:31,480
The 2006 title went to the team that had finished third: Inter Milan.

145
00:11:32,030 --> 00:11:38,117
The decision was taken by Guido Rossi, the lawyer appointed to run the federation

146
00:11:38,167 --> 00:11:43,497
after the scandal, together with a panel of three experts. The problem?

147
00:11:43,547 --> 00:11:47,210
Rossi had once sat on Inter's board of directors.

148
00:11:47,760 --> 00:11:53,258
For Juventus fans, that detail alone made the decision suspicious forever.

149
00:11:53,308 --> 00:11:59,331
Even the former federation president, Franco Carraro, later said the title should

150
00:11:59,381 --> 00:12:02,780
have been taken away… but not given to anyone.

151
00:12:03,330 --> 00:12:09,552
Inter's president, Massimo Moratti, put it simply. "The 2006 title…

152
00:12:09,602 --> 00:12:11,330
I feel it is mine."

153
00:12:11,880 --> 00:12:16,437
Moggi and Giraudo were banned from football for five years.

154
00:12:16,487 --> 00:12:19,950
A ban that would later become a lifetime ban.

155
00:12:20,500 --> 00:12:23,451
And now, the world champions had to decide.

156
00:12:23,501 --> 00:12:27,290
Would they stay with their club in the second division?

157
00:12:31,840 --> 00:12:32,596
Some left.

158
00:12:32,646 --> 00:12:39,042
Cannavaro, captain of the world champions, joined Real Madrid, and at the end of

159
00:12:39,092 --> 00:12:44,038
the year he won the Ballon d'Or. Zambrotta left for Barcelona.

160
00:12:44,088 --> 00:12:47,020
Ibrahimović and Vieira went to Inter.

161
00:12:47,570 --> 00:12:52,892
But others stayed. Buffon, the best goalkeeper in the world.

162
00:12:52,942 --> 00:12:58,712
Del Piero, the club captain. Nedvěd, the 2003 Ballon d'Or winner.

163
00:12:58,762 --> 00:13:03,010
And Trezeguet, the man who had missed in Berlin.

164
00:13:03,560 --> 00:13:09,058
Asked why he stayed, Del Piero is credited with a line that became legend.

165
00:13:09,108 --> 00:13:15,056
"A true gentleman never leaves his lady." And the play on words was intentional.

166
00:13:15,106 --> 00:13:18,430
In Italy, Juventus are known as the Old Lady.

167
00:13:18,980 --> 00:13:22,597
Eighteen years later, Del Piero told the story himself.

168
00:13:22,647 --> 00:13:27,597
"When the owner called me, I said: don't need to ask me. I'm going to stay.

169
00:13:27,647 --> 00:13:32,197
I know what's going on… maybe we get relegated to the third division.

170
00:13:32,247 --> 00:13:33,863
And I said: I don't care.

171
00:13:33,913 --> 00:13:38,730
We won everything, and we're going to come back to win everything again."

172
00:13:39,280 --> 00:13:47,537
On the ninth of September 2006, Juventus played their first ever match in Serie B.

173
00:13:47,587 --> 00:13:50,880
Away, in Rimini. It finished 1–1.

174
00:13:51,430 --> 00:13:56,450
World champions… in stadiums that held fewer than ten thousand people.

175
00:13:57,000 --> 00:13:58,991
But the team did not break.

176
00:13:59,041 --> 00:14:04,662
They started the season nine points behind, and finished it in first place.

177
00:14:04,712 --> 00:14:08,896
Del Piero was the league's top scorer with twenty goals.

178
00:14:08,946 --> 00:14:11,240
Juventus went straight back up.

179
00:14:11,790 --> 00:14:17,613
And the biggest twist of all: Milan, one of the punished clubs, who started their

180
00:14:17,663 --> 00:14:23,487
season eight points down and had to play a qualifying round at the end of August…

181
00:14:23,537 --> 00:14:29,505
won the Champions League in Athens in May 2007, with two goals from Filippo Inzaghi

182
00:14:29,555 --> 00:14:30,810
against Liverpool.

183
00:14:35,360 --> 00:14:38,422
The football courts had delivered their verdict.

184
00:14:38,472 --> 00:14:40,950
The criminal courts were another story.

185
00:14:41,500 --> 00:14:48,489
In 2011, a court in Naples sentenced Moggi to five years and four months in prison.

186
00:14:48,539 --> 00:14:53,493
On appeal in 2013, it was cut to two years and four months.

187
00:14:53,543 --> 00:14:58,243
And in March 2015, Italy's highest court ended the case…

188
00:14:58,293 --> 00:15:01,720
because the legal time limit had run out.

189
00:15:02,270 --> 00:15:07,780
Moggi never went to prison. But his lifetime ban from football remained.

190
00:15:08,330 --> 00:15:13,949
Of all the referees whose names appeared in the case, only one was finally

191
00:15:13,999 --> 00:15:16,170
convicted: Massimo De Santis.

192
00:15:16,720 --> 00:15:23,143
But even after the case had expired, Italy's highest court published its written

193
00:15:23,193 --> 00:15:27,350
reasons in September 2015. And its words were harsh.

194
00:15:27,900 --> 00:15:34,355
It described Moggi as "the creator of an illicit system to condition the matches of

195
00:15:34,405 --> 00:15:36,549
the 2004–2005 championship."

196
00:15:36,599 --> 00:15:42,897
And it wrote: "More than power, we must speak of an overwhelming power, extending

197
00:15:42,947 --> 00:15:45,640
even to journalism and television."

198
00:15:46,190 --> 00:15:48,542
Then, in 2010, came a surprise.

199
00:15:48,592 --> 00:15:54,662
During the trial, Moggi's defence produced recordings that had not been used in

200
00:15:54,712 --> 00:15:55,050
2006.

201
00:15:55,100 --> 00:16:00,860
Calls between executives from other clubs, including Inter, and the referee

202
00:16:00,910 --> 00:16:01,790
designators.

203
00:16:02,340 --> 00:16:08,780
In one call, in February 2005, Facchetti tells Bergamo the name of the next referee

204
00:16:08,830 --> 00:16:13,940
for Inter's match. Bergamo replies: "Tell the coach to stay calm."

205
00:16:14,490 --> 00:16:18,388
In another, Bergamo invites him to dinner at his home.

206
00:16:18,438 --> 00:16:24,310
"We'll eat something together… a little fish… something simple, between friends."

207
00:16:24,860 --> 00:16:29,160
Were these innocent calls? Or the same thing Moggi had done?

208
00:16:29,210 --> 00:16:32,930
That is exactly the question no court ever answered.

209
00:16:33,480 --> 00:16:36,956
Moggi summed up his defence in one sentence.

210
00:16:37,006 --> 00:16:41,763
"If I call a designator to complain, and the rules allow it…

211
00:16:41,813 --> 00:16:47,853
and everyone else who called more than me and asked for favours is left out…

212
00:16:47,903 --> 00:16:52,340
then what I did, which is not a crime, becomes a crime."

213
00:16:52,890 --> 00:16:58,987
In 2011, federation prosecutor Stefano Palazzi wrote that Inter could have been

214
00:16:59,037 --> 00:17:03,110
charged with breaking the rules of sporting fairness.

215
00:17:03,160 --> 00:17:06,145
But the facts were already time-barred.

216
00:17:06,195 --> 00:17:12,525
And Inter's president at the time, Giacinto Facchetti, had died in September 2006,

217
00:17:12,575 --> 00:17:14,470
unable to defend himself.

218
00:17:15,020 --> 00:17:21,220
On the eighteenth of July 2011, Juventus asked the federation to take the 2006

219
00:17:21,270 --> 00:17:27,791
title away from Inter. The federation's council declared it had no power to do so.

220
00:17:27,841 --> 00:17:34,361
Its president, Giancarlo Abete, said he wished Inter had given up its right to the

221
00:17:34,411 --> 00:17:39,810
statute of limitations. They did not. The title stayed where it was.

222
00:17:40,360 --> 00:17:46,240
Juventus went to the administrative courts, claiming around four hundred and

223
00:17:46,290 --> 00:17:51,624
forty-four million euros in damages. In 2016, the claim was rejected.

224
00:17:51,674 --> 00:17:57,710
In 2023, seventeen years after the scandal, Juventus withdrew its last appeal.

225
00:18:02,260 --> 00:18:06,306
Twenty years have passed since the summer of 2006.

226
00:18:06,356 --> 00:18:11,632
And in Italian cafés, the same old question is still being asked.

227
00:18:11,682 --> 00:18:13,680
Who chooses the referees?

228
00:18:14,230 --> 00:18:17,913
For Juventus fans, Calciopoli was an injustice.

229
00:18:17,963 --> 00:18:22,043
For their rivals, it was justice that came too late.

230
00:18:22,093 --> 00:18:28,556
And for the courts, most of the cases ended not with acquittal, and not with final

231
00:18:28,606 --> 00:18:32,130
conviction… but simply with time running out.

232
00:18:32,680 --> 00:18:38,773
But one thing is beyond dispute. In a single summer, Italy lifted the World Cup…

233
00:18:38,823 --> 00:18:41,460
and brought down its greatest club.

234
00:18:42,010 --> 00:18:47,378
And in Italian football, nothing was ever quite the same after that summer.

235
00:18:47,428 --> 00:18:49,040
Thank you for watching.
